<!-- 8 -->
<section class="sec" id="sec-cpt">
  <div class="sec-h"><span class="sec-n">08</span><h2>CPT &amp; Vane Shear</h2></div>

  <div class="grid2">
    <div class="vcard v-teal">
      <span class="vtag">Cone Penetration Test</span>
      <h4>Push, don't hammer</h4>
      <p>A 60&deg; cone of 10 cm&sup2; area is pushed in at 20 mm/s while load cells continuously record <strong>cone resistance q<sub>c</sub></strong> and <strong>sleeve friction f<sub>s</sub></strong>. The friction ratio \( F_r = f_s/q_c \times 100\% \) identifies soil type: low ratio means sand, high ratio means clay.</p>
      <p class="small" style="margin-top:.5rem">Advantages: continuous profile rather than a reading every 1.5 m, repeatable, no operator effect. Disadvantage: brings up no sample, and cannot penetrate gravel.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="vcard v-amb">
      <span class="vtag">Vane Shear Test</span>
      <h4>Undrained strength, in place</h4>
      <p>A four-bladed vane is pushed into soft clay and rotated until the soil shears on a cylindrical surface. The peak torque gives undrained shear strength directly, with no sampling disturbance at all.</p>
      <div class="eq">\[ c_u = \frac&#123;T&#125;&#123;\pi\left(\frac&#123;d^2 h&#125;&#123;2&#125; + \frac&#123;d^3&#125;&#123;6&#125;\right)&#125; \]</div>
      <p class="small">Apply Bjerrum's correction factor &lambda; (which falls with plasticity index) before design use &mdash; the raw vane value is generally unconservative.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="why-matters">
    <span>&#9889;</span>
    <div><strong>They complement rather than compete.</strong> CPT gives a continuous, objective stratigraphy; boreholes with SPT and Shelby tubes give you material you can actually test in a laboratory. Serious investigations use both &mdash; CPT to map the layers, boreholes to characterise them.</div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 9 -->
<section class="sec" id="sec-log">
  <div class="sec-h"><span class="sec-n">09</span><h2>The Boring Log</h2></div>

  <p>The borehole log is the deliverable that every later chapter consumes. It must record depth and description of each stratum, sample type and depth, SPT N values, groundwater level (both on encountering it and after standing), and the drilling method.</p>

  <div class="diagram-card">
    <h4>A Typical Log</h4>
    <svg viewBox="0 0 330 200" class="static-svg" style="max-width:330px">
      <rect x="40" y="20" width="46" height="34" fill="rgba(212,169,74,0.20)" stroke="var(--bdr2)"/>
      <rect x="40" y="54" width="46" height="56" fill="rgba(120,120,140,0.20)" stroke="var(--bdr2)"/>
      <rect x="40" y="110" width="46" height="46" fill="rgba(78,201,176,0.18)" stroke="var(--bdr2)"/>
      <rect x="40" y="156" width="46" height="26" fill="rgba(160,160,170,0.30)" stroke="var(--bdr2)"/>
      <text x="92" y="40" font-size="9" fill="var(--txt2)">Fill / topsoil</text>
      <text x="92" y="84" font-size="9" fill="var(--txt2)">Soft grey CLAY — Shelby @ 3 m</text>
      <text x="92" y="136" font-size="9" fill="var(--txt2)">Medium dense SAND — N = 18</text>
      <text x="92" y="172" font-size="9" fill="var(--txt2)">Weathered rock</text>
      <path d="M 30 66 l 6 -8 l 6 8 z" fill="#4a9fe0"/>
      <text x="4" y="72" font-size="8" fill="#4a9fe0">GWT</text>
      <text x="24" y="26" font-size="8" fill="var(--mut)" text-anchor="end">0 m</text>
      <text x="24" y="115" font-size="8" fill="var(--mut)" text-anchor="end">5 m</text>
      <text x="24" y="186" font-size="8" fill="var(--mut)" text-anchor="end">9 m</text>
    </svg>
    <p class="cap">Record what you observed, not what you concluded. Interpretation belongs in the report; the log is evidence, and it will be read years later by people who were not on site.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 10 -->
<section class="sec" id="sec-examples">
  <div class="sec-h"><span class="sec-n">10</span><h2>Worked Examples</h2></div>

  <div class="example">
    <div class="ex-h"><span>Example 1</span><h4>Full SPT Correction Chain</h4></div>
    <p class="prob"><strong>Problem:</strong> A field SPT in sand at 6 m depth gives N = 18. A safety hammer with rope and pulley was used (&eta;<sub>H</sub> = 60%), borehole 150 mm (&eta;<sub>B</sub> = 105%), rods 5 m (&eta;<sub>R</sub> = 85%), standard sampler (&eta;<sub>S</sub> = 100%). Effective overburden at that depth is 95 kPa. Find N<sub>60</sub>, (N<sub>1</sub>)<sub>60</sub>, &phi;' and D<sub>r</sub>.</p>
    <div class="steps">
      <div class="step"><b>1</b><div><strong>Energy correction:</strong> \( N_&#123;60&#125; = \dfrac&#123;18 \times 60 \times 1.05 \times 1.00 \times 0.85&#125;&#123;60&#125; = 16.1 \)</div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>2</b><div><strong>Overburden factor:</strong> \( C_N = \sqrt&#123;100/95&#125; = 1.026 \)</div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>3</b><div><strong>Normalised value:</strong> \( (N_1)_&#123;60&#125; = 1.026 \times 16.1 = 16.5 \)</div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>4</b><div><strong>Friction angle:</strong> \( \phi' = 27.1 + 0.3(16.5) - 0.00054(16.5)^2 = 31.9^\circ \)</div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>5</b><div><strong>Relative density:</strong> \( D_r = \sqrt&#123;16.5/60&#125; = 0.52 = 52\% \) &mdash; medium dense</div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="ans"><strong>Answer:</strong> N<sub>60</sub> = 16.1, (N<sub>1</sub>)<sub>60</sub> = 16.5, &phi;' &asymp; 32&deg;, D<sub>r</sub> &asymp; 52% (medium dense). Note the raw N = 18 would have overstated the soil; the corrections matter.</div>
  </div>

  <div class="example">
    <div class="ex-h"><span>Example 2</span><h4>Depth of Boring</h4></div>
    <p class="prob"><strong>Problem:</strong> A 6-storey reinforced concrete building with a relatively wide footprint is planned. Estimate the required borehole depth using Sowers' rule.</p>
    <div class="steps">
      <div class="step"><b>1</b><div>Heavy concrete building with wide spacing &rarr; use C = 6 m.</div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>2</b><div>\( D_b = C S^&#123;0.7&#125; = 6 \times 6^&#123;0.7&#125; \). Since \( 6^&#123;0.7&#125; = 3.51 \), \( D_b = 21.1\text&#123;m&#125; \)</div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>3</b><div><strong>Cross-check with the stress rule:</strong> bore until \( \Delta\sigma \le 0.1q \). For a wide loaded area this typically occurs at roughly twice the foundation width &mdash; and whichever criterion gives the greater depth governs.</div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>4</b><div><strong>Judgement:</strong> if soft compressible clay is still being logged at 21 m, keep going. The rules give a starting depth, not permission to stop.</div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="ans"><strong>Answer:</strong> About 21 m as a first estimate, extended if weak strata continue below that depth.</div>
  </div>

  <div class="example">
    <div class="ex-h"><span>Example 3</span><h4>Is This Sampler Good Enough?</h4></div>
    <p class="prob"><strong>Problem:</strong> A thin-walled tube has outside diameter 76.2 mm and inside diameter 73.0 mm. Compute the area ratio and state whether it is suitable for consolidation testing.</p>
    <div class="steps">
      <div class="step"><b>1</b><div>\( A_R = \dfrac&#123;D_o^2-D_i^2&#125;&#123;D_i^2&#125;\times100 = \dfrac&#123;76.2^2-73.0^2&#125;&#123;73.0^2&#125;\times100 \)</div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>2</b><div>\( = \dfrac&#123;5806.4-5329.0&#125;&#123;5329.0&#125;\times100 = \dfrac&#123;477.4&#125;&#123;5329.0&#125;\times100 = 8.96\% \)</div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>3</b><div>8.96% &lt; 10%, so disturbance is acceptable for undisturbed testing.</div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="ans"><strong>Answer:</strong> A<sub>R</sub> = 9.0% &mdash; suitable. For contrast, a standard split-spoon sampler has A<sub>R</sub> above 100%, which is why its samples can only ever be used for classification.</div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 11 -->
<section class="sec" id="sec-quiz">
  <div class="sec-h"><span class="sec-n">11</span><h2>Quick Reference &amp; Quick Check</h2></div>

  <div class="qref">
    <div><span>SPT hammer</span><span>63.5 kg falling 760 mm</span></div>
    <div><span>SPT N value</span><span>blows for 2nd + 3rd 150 mm</span></div>
    <div><span>Energy correction</span><span>N&#8326;&#8320; = N&eta;<sub>H</sub>&eta;<sub>B</sub>&eta;<sub>S</sub>&eta;<sub>R</sub>/60</span></div>
    <div><span>Overburden factor</span><span>C<sub>N</sub> = &radic;(p<sub>a</sub>/&sigma;'&#8320;), sand only</span></div>
    <div><span>Relative density</span><span>D<sub>r</sub> &asymp; &radic;((N&#8321;)&#8326;&#8320;/60)</span></div>
    <div><span>Friction angle</span><span>&phi;' &asymp; 27.1 + 0.3(N&#8321;)&#8326;&#8320; &minus; 0.00054(N&#8321;)&#8326;&#8320;&sup2;</span></div>
    <div><span>Area ratio</span><span>(D<sub>o</sub>&sup2;&minus;D<sub>i</sub>&sup2;)/D<sub>i</sub>&sup2; &times; 100 &le; 10%</span></div>
    <div><span>Sowers' depth rule</span><span>D<sub>b</sub> = C&middot;S<sup>0.7</sup></span></div>
    <div><span>Stress depth rule</span><span>bore until &Delta;&sigma; &le; 0.1q</span></div>
    <div><span>CPT friction ratio</span><span>F<sub>r</sub> = f<sub>s</sub>/q<sub>c</sub> &times; 100%</span></div>
  </div>

  <div class="quiz" id="quiz">
    <div class="q" data-a="2">
      <p>1. Why is the blow count from the first 150 mm of SPT penetration discarded?</p>
      <button data-v="0">It is always zero</button>
      <button data-v="1">To make the test faster</button>
      <button data-v="2">That zone is disturbed by the drilling that created the borehole</button>
    </div>
    <div class="q" data-a="1">
      <p>2. Two identical sands are tested, one at 3 m and one at 15 m. The deeper gives a much higher N. What does the overburden correction do?</p>
      <button data-v="0">Increases the deeper value further, since deep soil is stronger</button>
      <button data-v="1">Normalises both to a common reference stress so their densities can be compared</button>
      <button data-v="2">Nothing — it applies only to clay</button>
    </div>
    <div class="q" data-a="0">
      <p>3. You need C<sub>c</sub> and c<sub>v</sub> for a settlement analysis. Which sample will do?</p>
      <button data-v="0">A Shelby tube sample — consolidation needs undisturbed structure</button>
      <button data-v="1">A split-spoon sample — it comes from the right depth</button>
      <button data-v="2">Either; consolidation parameters are unaffected by disturbance</button>
    </div>
    <div class="q" data-a="1">
      <p>4. Drilling meets refusal at 8 m. Is it safe to conclude bedrock has been reached?</p>
      <button data-v="0">Yes — refusal is the standard definition of bedrock</button>
      <button data-v="1">No — it may be a boulder or cemented lens; core several metres to confirm</button>
      <button data-v="2">Yes, provided the SPT N value exceeds 50</button>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div id="quiz-score" class="quiz-score"></div>
</section>

<footer class="chapter-nav">
  <a href="/civil/cee340/settlement">&larr; Settlement Computations</a>
  <a href="/civil/cee340/shallow-foundations">Next: Shallow Foundations &rarr;</a>
</footer>

<script is:inline>
(function(){
  const g=id=>document.getElementById(id), v=id=>+g(id).value;

  function spt(){
    const N=v('sp-n'), eH=v('sp-eh'), eB=v('sp-eb'), eR=v('sp-er'), eS=v('sp-es');
    const sv=v('sp-sv'), sand=g('sp-soil').value==='sand';
    if(N<=0||sv<=0){ g('sp-res').innerHTML='<div class="verdict v-fail"><span>Check inputs</span></div>'; return; }
    const N60 = N*(eH/100)*(eB/100)*(eS/100)*(eR/100)*100/60;
    const CN = Math.min(1.7, Math.sqrt(100/sv));
    const N160 = sand ? CN*N60 : null;
    let rows =
      `<div class="res"><span>Raw field N</span><b>${N}</b></div>`+
      `<div class="verdict v-pass"><span>Energy corrected N&#8326;&#8320;</span><b>${N60.toFixed(1)}</b></div>`;
    if (sand){
      const Dr = Math.min(100, 100*Math.sqrt(Math.max(0,N160)/60));
      const phi = 27.1 + 0.3*N160 - 0.00054*N160*N160;
      let desc = N160<4?'Very loose':N160<10?'Loose':N160<30?'Medium dense':N160<50?'Dense':'Very dense';
      rows += `<div class="res"><span>Overburden factor C<sub>N</sub></span><b>${CN.toFixed(3)}</b></div>`+
        `<div class="verdict v-pass"><span>Normalised (N&#8321;)&#8326;&#8320;</span><b>${N160.toFixed(1)}</b></div>`+
        `<div class="res"><span>Friction angle &phi;'</span><b>${phi.toFixed(1)}&deg;</b></div>`+
        `<div class="res"><span>Relative density D<sub>r</sub></span><b>${Dr.toFixed(0)}% — ${desc}</b></div>`;
    } else {
      const cu = 6*N60;
      let desc = N60<2?'Very soft':N60<4?'Soft':N60<8?'Medium':N60<15?'Stiff':N60<30?'Very stiff':'Hard';
      rows += `<div class="res"><span>Overburden correction</span><b>not applied (clay)</b></div>`+
        `<div class="res"><span>Approx. c<sub>u</sub> &asymp; 6N&#8326;&#8320;</span><b>${cu.toFixed(0)} kPa — ${desc}</b></div>`+
        `<div class="verdict v-fail"><span>For clay, sample and test — do not design on correlation</span><b>!</b></div>`;
    }
    g('sp-res').innerHTML=rows;

    // bar chart: N -> N60 -> (N1)60
    const bars = sand ? [['N',N,'var(--mut)'],['N₆₀',N60,'var(--teal)'],['(N₁)₆₀',N160,'var(--amb)']]
                      : [['N',N,'var(--mut)'],['N₆₀',N60,'var(--teal)']];
    const mx = Math.max(...bars.map(b=>b[1]),1);
    const W=280,Hh=140,bw=44,base=110;
    let svg='';
    bars.forEach((b,i)=>{
      const h=(b[1]/mx)*82, x=40+i*72;
      svg+=`<rect x="${x}" y="${base-h}" width="${bw}" height="${h}" fill="${b[2]}" opacity=".35" stroke="${b[2]}"/>`+
           `<text x="${x+bw/2}" y="${base-h-5}" font-size="10" fill="${b[2]}" text-anchor="middle">${b[1].toFixed(1)}</text>`+
           `<text x="${x+bw/2}" y="${base+13}" font-size="9" fill="var(--txt2)" text-anchor="middle">${b[0]}</text>`;
    });
    g('sp-fig').innerHTML=`<svg viewBox="0 0 ${W} ${Hh}" style="width:100%;height:auto">
      <line x1="30" y1="${base}" x2="${W-15}" y2="${base}" stroke="var(--bdr2)"/>${svg}</svg>`;
  }
  ['sp-n','sp-eh','sp-eb','sp-er','sp-es','sp-sv','sp-soil'].forEach(id=>g(id).addEventListener('input',spt));
  spt();

  document.querySelectorAll('.quiz .q').forEach(q=>{
    const btns=q.querySelectorAll('button');
    btns.forEach(btn=>btn.addEventListener('click',()=>{
      if(q.classList.contains('answered'))return;
      q.classList.add('answered');
      btns.forEach(b=>{ if(b.dataset.v===q.dataset.a)b.classList.add('correct'); else if(b===btn)b.classList.add('wrong'); });
      const a=document.querySelectorAll('.quiz .q.answered').length;
      const r=document.querySelectorAll('.quiz .q.answered button.correct').length;
      g('quiz-score').textContent=r+' / '+a+' answered correctly so far';
    }));
  });
})();
</script>

</Base>
